      "id": "MIN-BYPASS-013",
      "severity": "MIN",
      "claim": "The reviewer independence claim (§4.3: 'Reviewers do not see the fix-round narrative — only the resulting code') has a subtle bleed channel: Codex addressing consolidated findings may embed finding IDs or remediation descriptions in code comments, anchoring subsequent reviewers toward leniency on incompletely-fixed issues.",
      "evidence": "§4.3 reviewer isolation protects reviewers from each other's verdicts. But fix-round Codex receives the consolidated findings list (CRIT-BYPASS-004) and may write code comments like '# CRIT-BYPASS-001: using allow-list to enforce scope pre-hoc' or test names like 'test_scope_boundary_enforced_pre_hoc_crit_bypass_001'. A reviewer in round 2 reading this comment knows CRIT-BYPASS-001 was addressed and is anchored toward rating the fix as adequate, rather than independently re-deriving whether the vulnerability is actually closed. This mirrors the wrong-direction convergence dynamic that D-023 rejected for chat forums: consensus on 'fixed' forms faster than genuine independent verification justifies.",
      "impact": "Over 5 fix rounds, reviewers increasingly see Codex's interpretation of prior findings in the code, not just the code itself. A CRIT finding that was correctly identified in round 1 but incompletely fixed may be re-rated as MIN in round 3 because a code comment claims remediation. The false-convergence rate increases with fix-round depth, and is highest for exactly the slices that require the most rounds (the hardest security issues).",
      "mitigation": "Add to all three reviewer prompt templates: 'You may encounter code comments referencing specific finding IDs (e.g., CRIT-BYPASS-NNN) or prior review rounds. Treat these as potential anchoring artifacts — independently assess whether the underlying vulnerability is closed, not whether the comment claims closure.' Also add to the fix-round Codex package instruction: 'Do not reference finding IDs, severity levels, or prior review rounds in code comments or test names. Address findings silently in code. Finding references belong in the commit message only.'"
