    {
      "id": "nit-BYPASS-014",
      "severity": "nit",
      "claim": "docs/STATUS.md is not explicitly listed in the scope_boundary partition table, and the mitigation claim that it is only touched 'at slice merge time, not during Codex execution' is unenforceable: any scope_boundary glob matching 'docs/**' would include STATUS.md and allow Codex to modify it during execution without triggering a scope_violation.",
      "evidence": "WP-SCP-022 §4.4 and R-022-04: 'STATUS.md is touched only at slice merge time, not during Codex execution.' The scope_boundary enforcement is post-hoc (CRIT-BYPASS-001) and is glob-based — if any slice's scope_boundary contains 'docs/**' or 'docs/STATUS.md' explicitly, Codex modifying STATUS.md during execution would not be a scope violation. The plan does not list STATUS.md in a global exclusion list or require that dispatch packages explicitly exclude it. Documentation-heavy slices (020H part 3 ADOPT-001 appendix, 021F ADOPT-001 §13) are plausible candidates for 'docs/**' scope globs.",
      "impact": "Both tracks concurrently touching docs/STATUS.md during Codex execution (not just at merge time) produces a merge conflict or silent data loss when the second track's PR rebases. Low blast-radius but audit-trail polluting — STATUS.md is the estate's canonical state summary and incorrect entries could propagate stale information to downstream agents reading it.",
      "mitigation": "Add docs/STATUS.md and docs/DECISIONS.md to an explicit global exclusion list in the dispatch-package authoring invariants: 'No slice's scope_boundary may match docs/STATUS.md or docs/DECISIONS.md via glob (these files are modified only by the orchestrator at merge time, not by Codex during slice execution).' Make this a failure criterion for the completeness_governance review lens: any scope_boundary glob that would match these files causes the reviewer to flag a MIN finding."
